## Proof

**Claim:** The minimal polynomial of a Pisot number is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$.

**Answer: Yes.** In fact, this holds for *every* algebraic number, not just Pisot numbers — it is a consequence of the very definition of "minimal polynomial."

**Definitions and setup.**

A **Pisot number** (Pisot–Vijayaraghavan number) is a real algebraic integer $\theta > 1$ all of whose Galois conjugates $\theta' \neq \theta$ satisfy $|\theta'| < 1$. In particular, $\theta$ is an algebraic number.

Let $\theta$ be any algebraic number (hence in particular any Pisot number). Its **minimal polynomial over $\mathbb{Q}$** is defined as the monic polynomial $m_\theta(x) \in \mathbb{Q}[x]$ of least positive degree such that $m_\theta(\theta) = 0$.

**Key fact.** The minimal polynomial $m_\theta(x)$ is irreducible in $\mathbb{Q}[x]$.

*Proof of the key fact.* Suppose for contradiction that $m_\theta(x)$ is reducible over $\mathbb{Q}$, so $m_\theta(x) = f(x)\,g(x)$ with $f, g \in \mathbb{Q}[x]$ both of positive degree strictly less than $\deg m_\theta$. Evaluating at $\theta$:

$$0 = m_\theta(\theta) = f(\theta)\,g(\theta),$$

so $f(\theta) = 0$ or $g(\theta) = 0$. Whichever factor vanishes at $\theta$ is a monic (after rescaling) polynomial in $\mathbb{Q}[x]$ of degree *strictly less* than $\deg m_\theta$ having $\theta$ as a root. This contradicts the defining property of $m_\theta$ as the least-degree such polynomial. Hence $m_\theta(x)$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$. $\square$

(Equivalently, $m_\theta$ generates the principal ideal $\ker(\mathrm{ev}_\theta : \mathbb{Q}[x] \to \mathbb{Q}(\theta))$, which is prime since $\mathbb{Q}(\theta)$ is an integral domain; a prime principal ideal in the PID $\mathbb{Q}[x]$ is generated by an irreducible element.)

**Conclusion.** Since a Pisot number is, in particular, an algebraic number, its minimal polynomial over $\mathbb{Q}$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the general argument above. The Pisot property (conjugates inside the unit disk) plays no role in the irreducibility — it only constrains *which* irreducible polynomials can arise as minimal polynomials of Pisot numbers (namely, those with exactly one real root $>1$ and all other roots of modulus $<1$).

